WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.110 --> 00:00:09.030
You can imagine

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:13.030
that the oceans are actually
the largest battery.

00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:17.080
We're storing huge amounts
of energy in the oceans.

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:19.880
The wave motion can be very deep.

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:22.710
It can extend down several hundred meters,

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:26.830
and once it gets to the near
shore, from about 50 meters,

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:29.663
the whole water column is
moving backwards and forwards.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:34.200
As we search for ways
to reduce our reliance

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:36.380
on fossil fuels, some are looking

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:40.400
to a largely untapped potential
source of renewable energy.

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:44.260
In theory, waves off the coast
of the United States alone

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:46.960
could generate over two
trillion kilowatt hours

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:48.640
of electricity a year,

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:51.310
enough to power more
than half of the country.

00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:55.270
Waves intensify and subside
not as quickly as the wind,

00:00:55.270 --> 00:00:56.670
and that means that it can produce

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:58.550
a smoother power generation curve.

00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:01.820
One of the potential
advantages of wave energy is

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:06.070
that it could act as a
complementary source of power,

00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:08.130
compared to other renewables on the grid

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:11.273
if it were to be scaled
up to a commercial scale.

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:19.020
For decades, engineers have been trying

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:22.010
to convert wave energy into electricity

00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:23.610
but a host of technological

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:27.130
and financial challenges have
complicated their efforts.

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:31.120
In the years from around 2006 to 2015,

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:33.930
there were a spate of
bankruptcies in the sector,

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:36.460
and this was largely because of a lack

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:40.540
of kind of continuous
proven projects out at sea

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:42.833
with reliable electricity generation.

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:46.130
Since then, companies have been trying

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:48.840
to develop the technologies
at a steadier pace

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:51.880
and with a smaller cash burn.

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:54.030
But the same questions remain.

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:55.760
Can companies develop devices

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:58.200
and technologies that actually work?

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:00.900
Is wave energy just a novelty or something

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:03.553
that can become a major
renewable energy source?

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:13.180
The sea is a very challenging environment

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:15.290
within which to operate a power project.

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:18.690
So seawater is corrosive and
conditions are very rough.

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:20.360
So this means that power projects

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:21.880
don't have a very long lifetime

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:25.210
and it increases operating
and maintenance costs.

00:02:25.210 --> 00:02:26.450
Partly because of that,

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:29.440
many early wave energy
projects hit rough waters,

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:32.830
such as the Pelamis wave
energy converter in Portugal

00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:35.730
and the Islay LIMPET project in Scotland,

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:38.023
but that hasn't stopped
others from trying.

00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:46.000
Companies are focused on
testing projects out at sea,

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:50.370
proving their durability,
trying to raise capital

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:52.260
and bring down cost.

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:54.710
The sector hasn't really converged

00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:57.580
around one single technology design

00:02:57.580 --> 00:02:59.950
and companies are kind of undecided

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:01.863
about which design works best.

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:15.360
Finland-based AW Energy is
a veteran in the sector.

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:18.830
The first proof of concept
of the company's device

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:22.750
was made in the '90s after
diver Rauno Koivusaari

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:25.990
observed the strong back and
forth movement of a hatch cover

00:03:25.990 --> 00:03:28.200
in a shipwreck in the Baltic Sea.

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:31.410
The waves are generated
far from the coastline.

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:35.760
So the wind blows on
the surface of the water

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:38.670
causing the water particles to rotate.

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:42.630
And that rotation extends
deep down below the surface

00:03:42.630 --> 00:03:44.160
and the waves can be very long,

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:46.840
they can be several hundred meters long,

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:49.100
and as they come into the near shore,

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:53.200
this rotational energy turns
into an elliptical energy

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:54.203
and eventually backwards and forwards

00:03:54.203 --> 00:03:56.240
that you probably have noticed
if you've been swimming

00:03:56.240 --> 00:03:59.910
on the seashore in large waves,
you're pulled in and out.

00:03:59.910 --> 00:04:02.240
That's the energy that we're extracting

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:04.460
but there's a sweet spot where we deploy.

00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:07.680
So around 10 to 15 meters of water depth,

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:10.900
that's where there's still
strong wave energy coming in.

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:14.200
After years of research,
prototyping and testing,

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:16.720
the company has deployed WaveRoller,

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:20.980
a 350-kilowatt device in
the waters off Portugal.

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:23.040
WaveRoller has an 18-meter wide

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:26.070
and 10-meter high steel
panel fixed to the seabed

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:28.180
via a floatable foundation.

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:30.610
The panel moves back and
forth with the waves,

00:04:30.610 --> 00:04:32.080
capturing the energy.

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:35.280
It's submerged in the depth of 15 meters

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:38.430
so it's protected from the extreme waves.

00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:43.360
We generate electricity
by capturing the movement

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:45.640
with hydraulic circuitry

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:49.090
in a machine room underneath the surface.

00:04:49.090 --> 00:04:52.210
That hydraulic energy
we turn to electricity

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:55.910
with hydraulic accumulators
and hydraulic motors,

00:04:55.910 --> 00:04:57.713
and also a generator.

00:04:58.628 --> 00:05:01.120
The WaveRoller has
survived large waves at sea

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:03.610
for over a year and delivered electricity

00:05:03.610 --> 00:05:06.470
via an underwater cable
to the grid in Portugal.

00:05:06.470 --> 00:05:10.390
Meanwhile, the company has
won a 2.5 million Euro grant

00:05:10.390 --> 00:05:13.420
to work on an upgraded
version of the WaveRoller,

00:05:13.420 --> 00:05:16.410
aiming to increase the
electricity generation capacity

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:17.860
to one megawatt.

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:20.350
The upscaled device would
have a bigger panel,

00:05:20.350 --> 00:05:21.980
two power takeoff units,

00:05:21.980 --> 00:05:25.150
and improved software to
control the energy production.

00:05:25.150 --> 00:05:30.150
We have taken into use a
wave prediction algorithm

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:33.880
that kind of tells us what kind of waves

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:36.670
are coming in to our device.

00:05:36.670 --> 00:05:40.510
That gives us a few seconds to prepare

00:05:40.510 --> 00:05:42.990
for capturing more energy

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:45.920
and the difference in that is significant.

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:50.090
Our future plans are to deliver technology

00:05:50.090 --> 00:05:51.820
around the world.

00:05:51.820 --> 00:05:56.820
So I'm hoping we can be working
on delivering technology

00:05:57.290 --> 00:05:59.060
to projects in Asia

00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:02.533
and also in the American
continent as well.

00:06:08.790 --> 00:06:11.810
Meanwhile, Israeli company Eco Wave Power

00:06:11.810 --> 00:06:13.250
is taking a different approach

00:06:13.250 --> 00:06:15.580
to capturing the power of the ocean.

00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:18.150
For the company's founder, Inna Braverman,

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:20.510
developing new sources of renewable energy

00:06:20.510 --> 00:06:22.310
is a personal mission.

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:25.260
I was born in Ukraine in 1996

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:26.760
and two weeks after I was born,

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:29.270
the Chernobyl nuclear reactor exploded,

00:06:29.270 --> 00:06:32.750
causing the largest in
history nuclear disaster.

00:06:32.750 --> 00:06:34.640
I was one of the babies that got hurt

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:37.390
from the negative effects
of such explosion.

00:06:37.390 --> 00:06:41.000
I had a respiratory arrest
and a clinical death.

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:44.920
Luckily my mother, a nurse,
approached my crib one time

00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:47.290
and gave me mouth-to-mouth resuscitation

00:06:47.290 --> 00:06:49.210
which saved my life.

00:06:49.210 --> 00:06:50.870
I got a second chance in life

00:06:50.870 --> 00:06:53.303
and decided to do something good with it.

00:06:54.690 --> 00:06:57.120
Growing up, Inna wanted to be a politician

00:06:57.120 --> 00:06:59.400
to positively change the world.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:02.880
After working as a translator
at a renewable energy company,

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:04.723
she decided to change paths.

00:07:06.490 --> 00:07:09.160
Solar and wind energy
were fully commercialized.

00:07:09.160 --> 00:07:11.250
There were a lot of amazing technologies

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:13.070
implemented everywhere.

00:07:13.070 --> 00:07:14.705
I saw that wave energy,

00:07:14.705 --> 00:07:17.900
although it's an immense
source of electricity,

00:07:17.900 --> 00:07:19.940
the biggest companies in the world

00:07:19.940 --> 00:07:23.680
are trying to develop wave
energy with no success.

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:26.400
And there was kind of a
race going on in the world

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:28.660
of who is the company
that is going to develop

00:07:28.660 --> 00:07:30.913
a viable wave energy solution first.

00:07:32.199 --> 00:07:35.200
In 2011, the then 24-year-old Inna

00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:37.363
co-founded Eco Wave Power.

00:07:38.470 --> 00:07:41.000
Instead of installing devices offshore,

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:42.790
the company's devices are attached

00:07:42.790 --> 00:07:45.980
to existing breakwaters,
jetties and piers.

00:07:45.980 --> 00:07:48.250
Our technology is very cost efficient,

00:07:48.250 --> 00:07:51.350
especially in comparison to
the offshore technologies,

00:07:51.350 --> 00:07:54.180
because we don't need any ships, divers,

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:56.300
underwater mooring or cables.

00:07:56.300 --> 00:07:58.710
We install on existing manmade structures

00:07:58.710 --> 00:08:01.620
and all our expensive
equipment, the generator,

00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:04.240
the hydraulic conversion
unit, the automation,

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:07.840
is located on land, just
like a regular power station.

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:11.210
This is the Eco Wave Power conversion unit

00:08:11.210 --> 00:08:16.198
that has been operating in Jaffa
port since 2014 until 2020.

00:08:17.330 --> 00:08:20.350
Here you can see how the technology works.

00:08:20.350 --> 00:08:23.410
Basically the floaters
are going up and down

00:08:23.410 --> 00:08:25.550
and pushing the hydro cylinders

00:08:25.550 --> 00:08:27.990
which transmit biodegradable fluid

00:08:27.990 --> 00:08:30.450
into land-located accumulators.

00:08:30.450 --> 00:08:31.980
A pressure is being built,

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:34.240
the higher the waves,
the higher the pressure,

00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:36.920
which is used to turn the hydro motor

00:08:36.920 --> 00:08:39.350
which is turning the generators.

00:08:39.350 --> 00:08:43.090
The whole system is controlled
by a smart automation system

00:08:43.090 --> 00:08:45.000
which you can see right here.

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:47.077
And in case of a coming storm,

00:08:47.077 --> 00:08:49.750
the system automatically
raises the floater

00:08:49.750 --> 00:08:53.850
above the water level and keeps
them in the upward position

00:08:53.850 --> 00:08:55.083
until the storm passes.

00:08:56.150 --> 00:08:57.610
The company has been operating

00:08:57.610 --> 00:09:02.400
a 100-kilowatt grid-connected
device in Gibraltar since 2016

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:05.270
which is enough to power 100 households.

00:09:05.270 --> 00:09:07.260
Currently Inna and her team are working

00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:09.470
on another 100-kilowatt project

00:09:09.470 --> 00:09:12.170
at the port of Jaffa in Tel Aviv.

00:09:12.170 --> 00:09:14.770
The project will be also the
first time in the history

00:09:14.770 --> 00:09:17.660
of Israel that wave energy
will officially connect

00:09:17.660 --> 00:09:19.030
to the electrical grid.

00:09:19.030 --> 00:09:22.100
And our goal going forward is to expand it

00:09:22.100 --> 00:09:24.990
to all suitable breakwaters
in the city of Tel Aviv

00:09:24.990 --> 00:09:26.940
and to supply a significant part

00:09:26.940 --> 00:09:28.803
of the city's electricity needs.

00:09:33.130 --> 00:09:35.240
Today, engineers and entrepreneurs

00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:37.453
are still trying to crack wave energy.

00:09:38.680 --> 00:09:40.870
Globally, only a few hundred kilowatts

00:09:40.870 --> 00:09:42.400
of wave energy are deployed

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:44.713
compared with gigawatts of offshore wind.

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:48.770
But the field has come a
long way in recent years,

00:09:48.770 --> 00:09:50.890
and with the right engineering solutions

00:09:50.890 --> 00:09:54.240
this new power source could
eventually become competitive

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:57.750
with more mature renewables,
like solar and wind.

00:09:57.750 --> 00:10:01.490
I would have a lot more
confidence in the success

00:10:01.490 --> 00:10:03.250
of wave energy companies today

00:10:03.250 --> 00:10:05.250
as opposed to, like, 20 years ago.

00:10:05.250 --> 00:10:08.370
But in the next few years,

00:10:08.370 --> 00:10:10.360
or maybe the next couple of decades,

00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:14.290
it will be more likely to
be used in remote locations,

00:10:14.290 --> 00:10:16.680
like islands, that are otherwise dependent

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:18.540
on expensive diesel power,

00:10:18.540 --> 00:10:21.850
or out at sea on gas decommissioning rigs,

00:10:21.850 --> 00:10:25.343
or for powering underwater
autonomous vehicles.

00:10:26.670 --> 00:10:29.400
So definitely wave energy can compete

00:10:29.400 --> 00:10:31.620
with other renewable energy sources,

00:10:31.620 --> 00:10:35.250
but I personally believe that
this is not a competition.

00:10:35.250 --> 00:10:39.010
In order to have a 100%
renewable energy friendly world,

00:10:39.010 --> 00:10:41.710
we need all renewable energy
sources to work together.

